# Line-ending policy for cross-platform (Linux + Windows/MSYS2) checkouts.
#
# The repository stores text with LF, and every checkout holds the same bytes
# whatever the cloning git's core.autocrlf says. Git for Windows installs with
# autocrlf=true and MSYS2's git without it, so without the first rule a clone
# made by one shows every text file as modified in the other (#119). Binaries
# are detected by text=auto and left alone.
#
# The shell scripts would break anyway with CR: a non-interactive bash under
# MSYS2 chokes on it ("$'\r': command not found"). The Windows scripts are the
# one exception, CRLF, for cmd.exe and PowerShell. A later line wins, so these
# exceptions stay below the default.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF (run by bash/sh under MSYS2 on Windows).
*.sh   text eol=lf

# Windows scripts stay CRLF.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf
